{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>Also with me HaShem was angered because of you, etc.</b> It is difficult: do we not find that HaShem was angered against Moses for the sin of the spies? And the Ramban explained, saying: 'And you added to do evil yet another time, until you caused anger against me' — end quote. But his words are not convincing, for it appears to me that he is still speaking about the matter of the spies, since Scripture says after this ״וְטַפְּכֶם אֲשֶׁר אֲמַרְתֶם״ etc. until the end of the passage, and he is speaking about the matter of the spies — so why did he interrupt within the subject with something not related to the subject? And as for the elegant explanation the Rav offered, that he wished to mention together those who were prevented from crossing into the land, since they were the cause of this — I do not know what benefit there is in this." "<b>And the correct interpretation</b> is according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 29a), who said regarding the verse (Numbers 14:1) ״וַיִּבְכּוּ הָעָם בַּלַּיְלָה״ etc. — they established weeping for generations, for it was the night of the ninth of Av, on which the Temple was destroyed. And they further said (Sotah 9a) that if Moses had entered the land and built the Holy Temple, the Temple would not have been destroyed, for no nation or tongue could touch it. And they further said (Midrash Tehillim 79) regarding the verse ״מִזְמוֹר לְאָסָף אֱלֹהִים בָּאוּ גוֹיִם״ etc., and this is their language: 'It should have said a lament (קִינָה)! But rather — because He cast His wrath upon wood and stones.' From this, if Moses had entered the land and built the Holy Temple, and He would have cast His wrath upon it — that would be impossible, as mentioned. And the wrath of HaShem would burn against the enemies of Israel, and their destruction would take the place of the Temple's destruction. Therefore HaShem decreed, in the decree of the spies, also upon Moses that he would die in the wilderness. And this is what the verse says: ״גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף ה׳ בִּגְלַלְכֶם״ — meaning, through the rolling-out (בְּגִלְגּוּל) of your affairs: for had there been no sin of the spies and they had entered [the land], it would have been possible for Moses to enter with them; and even though he would build the Holy Temple, there would be no concern, for the side of evil would not prevail and they would stand in their righteousness in the land. But through the severity of the sin of the spies, the hand of the impure forces prevailed, and HaShem knew that they would not stand in righteousness — as you may learn from the Song of Ha'azinu." "And if you should say: but it appears to me that the death of Moses was on account of the waters of Meribah (Numbers 20:13) — I have already written there that if Moses had sanctified His name, blessed be He, Israel would have returned to the purity they were in prior to the sin of the spies, through the agency of the great sanctification of the Name, and HaShem would have released Moses' oath, and he would have entered the land and built the Holy Temple as an eternal dwelling place."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:31.506337548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}